# Your Auggy application

Turn business operations into agent-ready capabilities. Auggy replaces one-off
integration glue with controlled, predictable TypeScript augments that bundle
identity, authorization, schemas, tools, routes and domain logic.

Install dependencies with `bun install` using Bun 1.3.14 or newer. Fill in the
provider placeholders in the private `.env`, then run `bun run typecheck`,
`bun test`, and `bun start`. Keep `.env` private; `.env.example` contains only
placeholders. The installed Auggy package must support application contract 1.
If startup reports an incompatible runtime, update the project dependency to an
application-capable release or the exact candidate package you are testing,
reinstall, and verify again. Files-only creation does not verify that installation.

`bun run dev` currently starts the same managed application as `bun start`.
`bun run cli` uses the local CLI and `{{CONFIG_FILE}}`. Neither script promises
watch or hot reload. After dependency installation, `auggy run` and `auggy dev`
also execute your declared application with the selected project-local runtime.

Follow a request through your source:

1. `src/index.ts` exports `main`, prepares composition, and starts the managed host.
2. `src/agent.ts` binds your local turn recipe to the augments declared in
   `{{CONFIG_FILE}}`; YAML is the single enabled-capability graph.
3. `src/http.ts` mounts `POST /agent/run` and passes the original request to the
   maintained adapter, which owns authentication, validation and durable delivery.
4. `src/turn.ts` dispatches internal work, prepares the prompt, runs inference and
   tools, handles required final answers, and finishes when the limit is exhausted.
5. Each guarded tool invocation reaches an installed built-in or your own narrow
   augment adapter. The runtime authorizes execution and settles history/accounting.

Edit these files to change application behavior. `tests/application.test.ts`
exercises recipe decisions without provider calls; run typecheck and tests after
edits. Configured inference limits remain effective. The omitted application
timeout currently means a 15-minute managed deadline. Dependency upgrades do not
regenerate these owned files. TypeScript is trusted application code; deterministic
authorization remains in guarded runtime operations and augment interfaces.

Create business adapters with `auggy augment create orders --with-skill`. See
`src/augments/README.md`. Root `skills/` is the single model-visible instruction
surface; keep secrets and runtime data out of it. Mutable state belongs in ignored
`data/`. Auggy does not replace application databases, durable workflows or systems
of record. Run `auggy inspect` for passive configuration and installed source
navigation; `auggy routes` executes application configuration and augments.
